<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">H. Rider Haggard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Henry Rider Haggard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir H. Rider Haggard was an English novelist who served in the colonial administration of Natal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> in the 1870s. His adventure novels set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, above all King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Mines (1885) and She (1887), were best-sellers that shaped Victorian images of the continent and founded the 'lost world' genre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/h-rider-haggard.jpg" alt="H. Rider Haggard" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Bain News Service, publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Rider_Haggard_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
